<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Barony of Vostitsa</h1><p class="st-meta">1209 CE – 1404 CE (approx.) · capital Vostitsa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Barony of the Principality of Achaea on the Gulf of Corinth, centred on Vostitsa, the modern Aigio.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the Frankish conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea</a> was divided into twelve baronies around 1209. Vostitsa, with a modest number of knight's fiefs, was granted to the Charpigny family from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Burgundy</a>, who held it through the 13th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The barons held the castle and town on the south shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a>, owed military service to the prince and sat in his high court, while Greek villagers farmed the land under them. Little detail of daily government survives beyond mentions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea">Chronicle of the Morea</a> and Angevin records.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 14th century the barony passed by inheritance and sale among several lords and was caught up in the struggles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navarrese-company">Navarrese Company</a>. It came to an end in the early 15th century, as the principality crumbled.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/barony-of-vostitsa.jpg" alt="Barony of Vostitsa" width="320" height="251"><figcaption>Image: Eric Gaba (Sting - fr:Sting); derivative work by Constantine · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peloponnese_Middle_Ages_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1209 CE – 1404 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vostitsa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Vostitsa, Aigio</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1306&amp;focus=barony-of-vostitsa" aria-label="Barony of Vostitsa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5230 804 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Barony of Vostitsa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5550 1060L5530 1071"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5530 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5550 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5550 1060h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1306&amp;focus=barony-of-vostitsa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

